00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:36.716
Well I’ve always been a history
buff. 

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:39.511
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
I’ve always been interested in
the origin of things, 

00:03:39.511 --> 00:03:41.012
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
why things are the way they are.

00:03:41.263 --> 00:03:43.348
Francine kept it honest as far
as the 

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:44.391
Antoine Fuqua:
wardrobe and the look. 

00:03:44.433 --> 00:03:47.060
♪♪

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.605
There were fabrics back then,
they were called “Negro cloth,” 

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:55.819
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
now called "Osnaburg," and it is
a rough grain, very scratchy. 

00:03:56.153 --> 00:04:01.867
So I like the fabric, but let’s
get something that is a bit more
comfortable. 

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:05.912
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
And that’s where I really wanted
to go with cotton and linen blend. 

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:08.540
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
Peter was running from these
vicious 

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.750
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
people who are trying to
capture him. 

00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:15.964
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
To go on that journey with him,
we actually had seventy shirts, 

00:04:16.423 --> 00:04:23.638
seventy trousers—all of these
things had to have a multiple of
seventy with his first outfit. 

00:04:24.139 --> 00:04:27.476
(Dog barks)

00:04:27.476 --> 00:04:34.524
The first time those iron
collars got put onto my neck, 

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:40.530
Will Smith:
it was like comprehension
slightly beyond your rational mind, 

00:04:40.614 --> 00:04:43.617
Will Smith:
where things come through
that you haven’t thought about. 

00:04:44.034 --> 00:04:48.330
Will Smith:
It was like stuck on my neck for
probably fifteen minutes. 

00:04:48.872 --> 00:04:54.628
People were running around
looking for a key to get it off
of my neck, 

00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:59.674
but Peter was being beaten while
he had it on his neck. 

00:04:59.758 --> 00:05:01.510
♪♪

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:04.971
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
To see my journey through this
industry 

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:09.059
Francine Jamison-Tanchuck:
as a Black woman, and a Black
American woman, 

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:13.104
I think my ancestors would be
very proud. 

00:05:13.563 --> 00:05:15.357
Antoine Fuqua:
Obviously we still deal with
things today, 

00:05:15.357 --> 00:05:18.902
but I think part of healing is
trying to find some truth in it.

00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:20.111
Antoine Fuqua:
And so that’s my journey. 

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:23.323
Antoine Fuqua:
That’s the journey that all my
crew came to join. 

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:24.741
Antoine Fuqua:
So hopefully that’s what we did.

00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:28.620
